WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.471 --> 00:00:05.009
Today, we're going to talk about
reconstructing evolutionary trees.

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:07.773
And the way that we're going to begin is

00:00:07.773 --> 00:00:11.982
with a story that may not seem
connected to evolutionary trees.

00:00:11.982 --> 00:00:14.609
This looks like a pretty typical hotel,
but

00:00:14.609 --> 00:00:19.089
it's actually the site of an important
moment in 21st Century history.

00:00:19.089 --> 00:00:23.394
It's in Hong Kong, and
it used to be called the Metropole Hotel.

00:00:23.394 --> 00:00:29.199
A Chinese doctor checked in to room 911
of this hotel on February 21, 2003.

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:33.560
He had been treating patients in
a rural area of mainland China,

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:37.374
who were suffering from
a deadly respiratory illness,

00:00:37.374 --> 00:00:39.276
and he had come to Hong Kong for
a wedding the next day.

00:00:39.276 --> 00:00:42.635
But when the next day came around,
he was too sick to attend, and

00:00:42.635 --> 00:00:46.784
he wound up checking himself into
a hospital down the street from the hotel.

00:00:46.784 --> 00:00:51.055
Two weeks later, he was dead.

00:00:51.055 --> 00:00:56.260
Now you're probably wondering,
"Why on earth would I tell this sad story?"

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:01.115
So let me travel back in time to the
14th Century when the Black Death

00:01:01.115 --> 00:01:03.599
killed a third of all Europeans.

00:01:03.599 --> 00:01:07.705
The Black Death was lethal,
but it was also very slow.

00:01:07.705 --> 00:01:12.925
It took four years for the Black Death
to travel from Constantinople

00:01:12.925 --> 00:01:15.145
(or modern day Istanbul) to Kiev.

00:01:15.145 --> 00:01:20.617
These days, you could do that trip by car
in just a day or just two hours by plane.

00:01:23.388 --> 00:01:27.964
In the 21st Century though, all it
takes for a disease to go global and

00:01:27.964 --> 00:01:32.923
become a crisis on the world scale is for
one person to travel from a an isolated

00:01:32.923 --> 00:01:37.601
place in rural China to a business
hotel in densely populated Hong Kong.

00:01:37.601 --> 00:01:40.831
Just on the ninth floor
of the Metropole Hotel,

00:01:40.831 --> 00:01:45.609
the Chinese doctor infected 13
other people from around the world.

00:01:45.609 --> 00:01:48.979
Two days after he checked in,
the disease was in Hanoi.

00:01:48.979 --> 00:01:52.182
Three days after that,
it crossed the Pacific to Toronto.

00:01:52.182 --> 00:01:54.952
Two days after that, it entered Singapore.

00:01:54.952 --> 00:01:56.100
In other words,

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:01.925
the disease was a global epidemic before
anybody realized it was a global epidemic.

00:02:01.925 --> 00:02:07.197
In fact, it was a global epidemic
before it even had a name.

00:02:07.197 --> 00:02:15.639
Today, we know this disease as
Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome, or SARS.

00:02:15.639 --> 00:02:20.541
So the bad news is that globalization
means that diseases travel

00:02:20.541 --> 00:02:22.546
fast in the 21 Century.

00:02:22.546 --> 00:02:27.090
But there are so many zombie movies out
there, on such widely varying topics as

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.220
Abraham Lincoln killing zombies, or

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.289
zombie beavers, that I hope
you all ready know that.

00:02:32.289 --> 00:02:37.102
In the bottom middle, there was
even a SARS inspired zombie movie

00:02:37.102 --> 00:02:41.932
that was based on "Star Wars" and
called -- believe it or not -- "SARS Wars".

00:02:41.932 --> 00:02:46.300
That's the bad news. The good news is
that we have a lot more weapons against

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:49.039
epidemics in the 21st
Century as well.

00:02:49.039 --> 00:02:52.404
Researchers quickly realized they
were dealing with the special

00:02:52.404 --> 00:02:56.513
type of virus called a coronavirus when
they looked at SARS under a microscope.

00:02:56.513 --> 00:02:58.582
Why is it called a coronavirus?

00:02:58.582 --> 00:03:02.980
Because when you look at
it under the microscope,

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:07.491
it looks like the sun's
corona during an eclipse.

00:03:07.491 --> 00:03:13.196
Now, coronaviruses, like HIV and
influenza are RNA viruses,

00:03:13.196 --> 00:03:16.900
which mean that they possess RNA not DNA.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:21.492
In 2003, researchers quickly
sequenced the SARS coronavirus,

00:03:21.492 --> 00:03:25.242
which makes up a genome
with 29,751 nucleotides.

00:03:25.242 --> 00:03:29.313
Here's a short snippet from
the end of this RNA genome.

00:03:29.313 --> 00:03:33.534
Now, RNA replication has a much
higher error rate than DNA,

00:03:33.534 --> 00:03:37.421
which means that RNA viruses
are able to mutate fast.

00:03:37.421 --> 00:03:43.318
This is why the flu shot changes from year
to year, and it's also why there are so

00:03:43.318 --> 00:03:48.966
many different types of HIV and
why we don't have a vaccine for HIV either.

00:03:48.966 --> 00:03:52.503
It's very difficult to vaccinate against
something that can mutate so quickly.

00:03:52.503 --> 00:03:56.140
Here we see it's mutated in just
in the time I'm talking to you.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:59.317
Now, research initially
thought that like HIV and

00:03:59.317 --> 00:04:02.814
influenza, SARS had jumped
from animals to humans.

00:04:02.814 --> 00:04:07.431
They first blamed birds, because of
the similarity of SARS with bird flu,

00:04:07.431 --> 00:04:10.583
a form of influenza that
originated in chickens and

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:14.191
first crossed the species
barrier to humans in 1997.

00:04:14.191 --> 00:04:17.661
But which animal did give us SARS?

00:04:17.661 --> 00:04:21.832
How was it that we came to be
infected in the first place?

00:04:21.832 --> 00:04:26.337
And how can we reconstruct its path
that it traveled around the world?

00:04:26.337 --> 00:04:31.309
It may not seem like it immediately, but
all of these questions are related to

00:04:31.309 --> 00:04:36.680
constructing evolutionary trees, which
are also commonly called phylogenies.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:40.527
For example,
here's the first evolutionary tree,

00:04:40.527 --> 00:04:43.954
which was drawn by Charles Darwin in 1837.

00:04:43.954 --> 00:04:48.459
And here's a phylogeny that's
constructed for HIV and its relatives.

00:04:48.459 --> 00:04:54.265
Just from this evolutionary tree,
we can infer that HIV crossed the species

00:04:54.265 --> 00:04:59.903
barrier from monkeys to humans at five
different places, marked by stars.

00:04:59.903 --> 00:05:05.809
These five different crossings divide
HIV into five different viral families.

00:05:05.809 --> 00:05:09.243
Now, from this HIV diagram,
it should be clear that if we want to

00:05:09.243 --> 00:05:13.802
know which animal gave us SARS, we should
just construct an evolutionary tree for

00:05:13.802 --> 00:05:17.588
the SARS coronavirus as well as
coronaviruses from other species.

00:05:17.588 --> 00:05:21.754
Then, we simply see where SARS is located
in this tree with respect to other

00:05:21.754 --> 00:05:22.793
coronaviruses.

00:05:22.793 --> 00:05:24.495
But the question remains though,

00:05:24.495 --> 00:05:28.260
what algorithms do we need to use in
order to construct evolutionary trees?

